content.theory:
Every element starts as `position: static` — it flows normally down the document, and `top`, `right`, `bottom`, and `left` have **no effect** on it. Static is the default, which is why you almost never type it explicitly. Real control begins with the other four values.

### relative
```css
.box {
  position: relative;
  top: 10px;
  left: 20px;
}
```
`relative` keeps the element in the normal flow but nudges it from its original spot using offsets. Crucially, it becomes a **positioning anchor** — it establishes a containing block for any absolutely positioned descendants.

### absolute
```css
.badge {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  right: 0;
}
```
`absolute` removes the element from the flow entirely (it no longer reserves space, so siblings may overlap it) and positions it relative to the **nearest positioned ancestor** — that is, the closest ancestor with `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, or `sticky`. If no ancestor is positioned, it anchors to the page root (`<html>`). This makes it the workhorse for badges, tooltips, and corner overlays. The classic pattern is wrapping the absolute element in a `relative` parent so the offsets apply to a predictable box.

### fixed and sticky
```css
.nav { position: fixed; top: 0; width: 100%; }
.aside { position: sticky; top: 12px; }
```
`fixed` pins an element to the **viewport** — it never moves when you scroll, ideal for navbars and floating action buttons (add padding to the page so it doesn't cover content). `sticky` is the hybrid: it scrolls with the page normally, but sticks as soon as it reaches its `top` value, staying put while you scroll past it. Sticky is the best choice for section headers and table headers that should remain visible.

### z-index & stacking contexts
When positioned elements overlap, they stack in DOM order by default — but you control the visual order with `z-index`: a higher number wins. `z-index` only works on **positioned** elements (and flex/grid items). Any element with a `z-index`, `opacity`, or `transform` also creates a **stacking context** that traps its children's z-index values inside it, so a modal at `z-index: 9999` nested inside a `z-index: 1` container still loses. Keep overlays at `10`, modals at `100`, and leave everything else at 0.

### When to use which

| Property | Use for |
|----------|---------|
| relative | nudging + anchoring absolutely positioned children |
| absolute | badges, tooltips, overlays inside a relative wrapper |
| fixed | viewport-pinned navbars, floating buttons |
| sticky | headers that stick after scrolling past them |

Most positioning bugs come from forgetting that `absolute` needs a positioned ancestor — if your badge lands in the wrong corner, walk up the DOM and check where its anchor chain starts.

